#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e04567-d2e1-5eef-aee3-62d784a9fe49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve Given the First Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "e2790549-46e6-51c2-b118-0c4a056f6d25" :question/math-academy-id "e-599" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x =e^{t}, \\, y= e^{2t}+1$. Given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dt}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t\n$$\n\nand we can calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} (e^t) = e^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(2e^{t}))/(e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2e^{t}}{e^{t}} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "68d38626-1153-51ec-a3c6-d39c927fdd99" :question/math-academy-id "e-600" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\sin(2t), \\qquad y= 2\\cos (2t) \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt \\pi$.\n\nGiven that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -2 \\tan(2t)$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac\\pi 8$." :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}\n$$\n\nwe can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dt}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -2 \\tan(2t)\n$$\n\nand we can calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} (\\sin 2t) = 2 \\cos(2t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\tan 2t))/(2\\cos (2t)) \\\\\n&= (-4\\sec^{2} (2t))/(2\\cos (2t)) \\\\\n&=-4\\sec^{2} (2t) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sec (2t) \\\\\n&=-2\\sec^{3} (2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} \\mid _{t = π/8} &= -2\\sec^{3} (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{8}) \\\\\n&=-2\\sec^{3} (\\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&=-2(\\sqrt{2})^{3} \\\\\n&=-4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068556 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "e621f828-5fc0-55b6-b726-f9da59878574" :question/math-academy-id "e-6803" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=e^t, y=2e^{2t}$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y$, we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=4e^{2t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{4e^{2t}}{e^t} = 4e^t\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dt}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(4e^{t}))/(e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4e^{t}}{e^{t}} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6037ca87-a84a-5b3b-b31c-f76225901a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263113} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c83168-f9f8-5298-b7c5-8d26af3cc218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263114} {:answer/id #uuid "5953b84f-786c-514c-a61e-2b10ad8d45f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263115} {:answer/id #uuid "91f85b9f-b136-5788-8649-639417f2d2a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263116} {:answer/id #uuid "73c4bbf4-e461-5111-9342-1de9ce64eaed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6c83168-f9f8-5298-b7c5-8d26af3cc218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263114} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8eb8cd0-fcac-5a6a-bc6e-25056dfbd68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c50ba4fe-b9c9-5f7a-86fc-f550f36e7831" :question/math-academy-id "q-122883" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{3}+6,\\,y=3{t}^{6}-3$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=18{t}^{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{18{t}^{5}}{3{t}^{2}}=6{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(6{t}^{3})}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18{t}^{2}}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58551232-4cd7-5304-9da7-e7205a64db20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{3}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263120} {:answer/id #uuid "d900f487-c790-5326-9698-a8d6c1ec56d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263121} {:answer/id #uuid "3d10414c-4cc7-59e3-ab2b-156e8ccb6ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{sec}^{3}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263122} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdf1d6b-bbde-5a61-b2cd-7671c0cefe61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263123} {:answer/id #uuid "d2587d2e-5436-5aa7-81b6-712649edaa08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{2}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d10414c-4cc7-59e3-ab2b-156e8ccb6ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{sec}^{3}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263122} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32006a1-f8d0-59e2-8316-3dbbd1ee1166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263119}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc3565c8-a104-503d-8d29-7c96e1c43097" :question/math-academy-id "q-122884" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-4,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+7.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)}=-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5t))}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10{sec}^{2}⁡(5t)}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{3}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b7da2b-24c5-5f48-84f0-c7179572b60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b7da2b-24c5-5f48-84f0-c7179572b60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263127} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba405bf-b8ee-5d87-a4a7-37dc89d05b25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "27423d28-32de-5814-8bee-7e569dad914a" :question/math-academy-id "q-352401" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-1.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ determine $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /9}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t))}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-(3{sec}^{2}⁡(3t)))}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{3}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /9}=-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{3}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{9})=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b61dcf-3032-532b-95a5-8bb56c7ba240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263130} {:answer/id #uuid "22ea0549-ce63-5505-bc83-5d96233889e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263131} {:answer/id #uuid "7badd336-4ad4-5759-bd84-aec9b4210cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263132} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fe2912-586b-5906-a5d7-59c61d4b80e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263133} {:answer/id #uuid "4b040bd1-31b4-569f-9d8a-95f3a508b0d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b040bd1-31b4-569f-9d8a-95f3a508b0d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263134} :answer-field/id #uuid "902af463-8279-57a4-91c7-95549b704ae8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263129}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7ac0d82-05be-587a-a9fe-883433fc2c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-50018" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t))}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(2t)}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{csc}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /12} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{csc}^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b53f892-54c3-52a7-9fda-582417133fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{5t-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263137} {:answer/id #uuid "a73e2a2e-6eb8-57a7-b1f4-cf435208cd18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{2-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263138} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d15f5d-393a-5cfe-a8fa-9a8969c06966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{5-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263139} {:answer/id #uuid "96ac62be-619d-5379-82f6-55197230c0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2+5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263140} {:answer/id #uuid "0c112eb4-a5c7-52c1-9112-49730dc21c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c112eb4-a5c7-52c1-9112-49730dc21c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263141} :answer-field/id #uuid "5975fea2-b841-5b0e-83b0-99ce0df0971c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fe821d1-e4ae-5970-baa0-08a1213478cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-50242" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=2{e}^{3t},\\,y=3{e}^{t+2}-5$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{e}^{t+2}}{6{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t})}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2-2t}}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e04567-d2e1-5eef-aee3-62d784a9fe49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve Given the First Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28389733-1de8-570b-8590-b9bb8c251cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263144} {:answer/id #uuid "20105136-6a19-51e8-bd01-aec2a01d5be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(2t-1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263145} {:answer/id #uuid "05426172-a9ec-53ca-863b-b7f197a79632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263146} {:answer/id #uuid "d07f2214-0058-580d-93e6-326018308fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(1-2t)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263147} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a33759-c7a4-5f93-bb25-550e76ef18ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-4t}(t+1)}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28389733-1de8-570b-8590-b9bb8c251cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263144} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd9e83a-f146-509e-8630-ab2da911ff7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263143}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "050a2f8c-6bed-5721-91c2-79fd18ca8372" :question/math-academy-id "q-50448" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-1,\\,y={e}^{-4t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}-1)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8t{e}^{-4t}+2{e}^{-4t}}{{t}^{2}})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8t{e}^{-4t}+2{e}^{-4t}}{2{t}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e04567-d2e1-5eef-aee3-62d784a9fe49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve Given the First Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c107e179-71f9-5052-84a4-3e85bec13d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263151} {:answer/id #uuid "2e8a5ebf-a596-57bb-9331-808088918bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263152} {:answer/id #uuid "c0df3cf6-3b98-5933-b422-f653f44c3e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263153} {:answer/id #uuid "8c71dd47-2da6-595d-87f7-09ee7600fcad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263154} {:answer/id #uuid "44c090a3-0893-5d2b-a845-f00d6138796d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c71dd47-2da6-595d-87f7-09ee7600fcad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263154} :answer-field/id #uuid "de339b16-3862-55a4-a661-c0c3acb47ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263150}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ab7cbb6-f2e2-58cc-98db-c0129f7267a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-54151" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}+2,\\,y={t}^{4}+{t}^{2}.$ Given  that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{t}^{2}+1,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{t}^{2}+1,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}+2)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{t}^{2}+1)}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{2t} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e04567-d2e1-5eef-aee3-62d784a9fe49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve Given the First Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192db562-a8ec-5299-b46e-f6f4ec5cf0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263158} {:answer/id #uuid "bf766ea7-32ad-5b36-8b00-76694dbd59f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263159} {:answer/id #uuid "94056c88-0506-583d-951b-90d047ab695e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263160} {:answer/id #uuid "03cdbd36-2edf-5dbc-b4d6-09eec7cbf4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263161} {:answer/id #uuid "3427ba36-813e-5057-b730-432994a34f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3427ba36-813e-5057-b730-432994a34f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263162} :answer-field/id #uuid "5105a368-e57e-551e-b644-dcef053e4c2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263157}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "386faf9d-04a8-5f56-a991-5f24aea9fe66" :question/math-academy-id "q-54152" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}},\\,y=\\frac{3}{t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}={t}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}={t}^{2},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{4}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2})}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{4}})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3882df83-607c-51ca-b92e-e0eaa5d30a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263165} {:answer/id #uuid "878a6a16-4a15-545e-b401-3eef30e753d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263166} {:answer/id #uuid "3937c7ac-f7d3-5d4a-a255-8ac71782e0e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263167} {:answer/id #uuid "c0430d4d-5389-5a3d-8a7c-82b0c0d31a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263168} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf3fc3d-7301-52a1-b30d-ed8372172b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3882df83-607c-51ca-b92e-e0eaa5d30a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263165} :answer-field/id #uuid "256c204f-4dbf-565e-a869-677c4b2cbb88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263164}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d10646a-979a-5514-8da1-2f5d756a9183" :question/math-academy-id "q-54154" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=2{e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{2t}.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}={e}^{t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{t})=2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{t})}{2{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ is constant, we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=3}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e41014f-1b86-5e63-877f-4a07d27c06b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263172} {:answer/id #uuid "cf676dd1-be35-5865-ba14-092df8b71770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263173} {:answer/id #uuid "90585cbf-3dc6-5e82-bce9-cc4a5f16a815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263174} {:answer/id #uuid "16f19e2e-e7f5-5d91-b14a-db8883ed7f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263175} {:answer/id #uuid "5330b9e8-3fce-5bd9-b7bf-6b491612e11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e41014f-1b86-5e63-877f-4a07d27c06b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263172} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e6ab48-b8e8-535f-b535-9593d3b4c967" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263171}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88279460-4fc3-5700-a3ab-5eacf5486340" :question/math-academy-id "q-54156" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,y={t}^{5}+2{t}^{3}$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}=5{t}^{2}+6.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(5{t}^{2}+6)}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e04567-d2e1-5eef-aee3-62d784a9fe49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve Given the First Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b514a03-a0d6-54e1-bbff-04a8df76e38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263179} {:answer/id #uuid "960633c2-1084-5301-b173-3b69a0e80f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡t}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263180} {:answer/id #uuid "c47751f5-0763-5b88-a45a-b6c0cde07d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263181} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0aaf5a-c4c4-5832-8a44-e8d73d15ebb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡t}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263182} {:answer/id #uuid "995ae3ee-48e0-5194-85c6-3b69874a1d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{csc}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b514a03-a0d6-54e1-bbff-04a8df76e38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263179} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5e5b19-24fd-561b-9b71-12af4aff7e71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263178}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1494994d-53d0-543a-9ac1-f8cdc36566af" :question/math-academy-id "q-54157" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{csc}^{2}⁡t}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =3{csc}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e04567-d2e1-5eef-aee3-62d784a9fe49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve Given the First Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159a2dfc-75eb-5e5b-b892-75adbe10cb82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263186} {:answer/id #uuid "143b5868-6a5f-5a54-bb58-c071931cb789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263187} {:answer/id #uuid "d352b1d8-ad41-55e7-a03b-df3caacaa6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263188} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4003aa-ab06-532a-aab4-6333d3321d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263189} {:answer/id #uuid "10bf38fe-6443-5ec4-a75c-a3da6bfe790b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10bf38fe-6443-5ec4-a75c-a3da6bfe790b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263190} :answer-field/id #uuid "acb254cf-4850-5842-a659-67353f7812eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263185}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "172db762-c9f7-5978-8014-91f8c5db0bad" :question/math-academy-id "q-54158" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={e}^{2t},\\,y={e}^{4t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{e}^{2t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{e}^{2t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{2t})=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{2t})}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62565470-b5f4-5119-ac64-e0232e6eb659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263193} {:answer/id #uuid "5c696b76-dde5-5867-8ae9-115bfa9b99ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263194} {:answer/id #uuid "b941c5dc-1432-55fa-a7fe-d25aa2aa548f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263195} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3fc8ed-c602-5144-937f-5a806e20dd5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263196} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae32ab5-ad4c-5ef4-8299-cc3379f387b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62565470-b5f4-5119-ac64-e0232e6eb659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263193} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ee3a2a-99d4-50c3-84c8-bee49548e1c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263192}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e35a5b10-6269-5d20-8a75-d8d74e499bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-78950" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t))}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00253b9e-c577-54d6-b759-e125aa598f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263200} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb361ed-c253-50db-ab52-823c55e6d93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a40fae-52b9-5c54-a0e7-62e3fab70180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263202} {:answer/id #uuid "218d5de1-f205-57d8-bebb-e96918be148f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263203} {:answer/id #uuid "6838aa97-146c-5ef6-87ff-c07376bb129d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cb361ed-c253-50db-ab52-823c55e6d93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} :answer-field/id #uuid "e266861a-b853-527c-b3c4-8c69ff94d2d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263199}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5716b83-ebe6-52ae-bc3e-c03ce4626188" :question/math-academy-id "q-78954" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2,\\,y={t}^{7}+2.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=7{t}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=7{t}^{2},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2)={t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(7{t}^{2})}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14t}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=2}=\\frac{14}{(2)^{3}}=\\frac{14}{8}=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98a9c757-1751-5193-ab36-03ab5ae69fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263207} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e5cbbd-914e-5335-8e99-3dccef9f4c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263208} {:answer/id #uuid "21bfd02d-25c9-502b-a94f-d0cb19f86f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263209} {:answer/id #uuid "a348a851-d127-5418-91c7-16d8c77a8423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263210} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f3f67f-95e4-52b2-8a33-4fe5d5ae774a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a348a851-d127-5418-91c7-16d8c77a8423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263210} :answer-field/id #uuid "0229aae6-6e30-5b04-8143-80c8504da636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263206}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b722008-9b33-571f-97bc-76e852fcdb22" :question/math-academy-id "q-78955" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{4}-5,\\,y=4{t}^{5}+2.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=5t,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=5t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{4}-5)=4{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(5t)}{4{t}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=1}=\\frac{5}{4(1)^{3}}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"}]}
